:::


From the sample, $\bar y = 297.9$ and $\bar x = 302$ thousand acres, so
$$
\hat B = \frac{\bar y}{\bar x} = 0.986565, \qquad
\hat{\bar y}_r = \hat B\,\bar x_U = 309.1, \qquad
\hat t_{yr} = \hat B\,t_x = 951,513 \text{ thousand acres}.
$$
The plain SRS estimate of the total, $N\bar y = 916,927$ thousand acres, is quite different, because the sampled counties happened to have $\bar x$ a little below $\bar x_U$. The ratio estimator corrects for this, and its standard error, 1.8, is about a tenth of the SRS standard error, 18.9.


::: {.cell}

```{.r .cell-code}
par(mar = c(4, 4, 0.5, 0.5))
plot(agsrs$acres87, agsrs$acres92, pch = 19, col = adjustcolor("grey30", 0.6), las = 1,
     xlab = "Farm acreage 1987 (thousands)", ylab = "Farm acreage 1992 (thousands)")
abline(0, Bhat_ag, col = "firebrick", lwd = 2)
```

::: {.cell-output-display}
![1992 against 1987 farm acreage for the SRS of 300 counties, with the ratio line $y = \hat B x$ through the origin. The spread about the line grows with $x$.](ratio_files/figure-html/fig-agsrs-acres8792-1.png){#fig-agsrs-acres8792 width=60%}
:::
:::


The points lie close to a line through the origin, and the variability about the line **increases with $x$**. This pattern, with the variance of $y$ roughly proportional to $x$, is exactly when ratio estimation works best.

